# Cloudflare Tunnel Integration & Configuration Guide

## Overview

This document outlines the architecture, configuration, and operational workflow for exposing a local **n8n** automation instance to the public internet securely using **Cloudflare Tunnels** (`cloudflared`).

By leveraging Cloudflare Tunnels, the n8n container running in a local WSL 2 / Docker Desktop environment can receive external webhook payloads and handle OAuth 2.0 authentication callbacks over a secure HTTPS endpoint—without opening router ports or exposing public IP addresses.

---

## 1. System Architecture

```text
[ External Webhook / Google OAuth ]
                │
                ▼
       [ Cloudflare Edge Network ]
                │
                ▼
 [ cloudflared daemon on Host ]
                │
                ▼
 [ Local HTTP Proxy / Docker Host ]
                │
                ▼
       [ n8n Container :5678 ]
```

---

## 2. Prerequisites & Environment Setup

- **Host Environment:** Windows 11 / WSL 2 with Docker Desktop
- **n8n Container:** Running on host port `5678`
- **Cloudflare CLI (`cloudflared`):** Installed on the host system
- **Cloudflare account:** Required for persistent named tunnels
- **Custom domain:** Required for a stable production hostname

---

## 3. Quick Tunnel Setup (Development / Testing)

Quick Tunnels generate a temporary HTTPS URL ending in `.trycloudflare.com`. They are useful for testing webhooks and OAuth callbacks without configuring a permanent Cloudflare Tunnel.

### 3.1 Establishing the Tunnel

Run the following command in PowerShell:

```powershell
cloudflared tunnel --protocol http2 --url http://localhost:5678
```

> **Note:** Forcing `--protocol http2` can be useful on Windows when QUIC/UDP transport causes confusing or incomplete console output.

### 3.2 Output Verification

Upon successful startup, `cloudflared` displays a temporary public endpoint similar to:

```text
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|  Your quick Tunnel has been created! Visit it at:                                         |
|  https://customise-dow-surprising-meet.trycloudflare.com                                   |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

The generated hostname is temporary and should be treated as a development/testing endpoint.

---

## 4. Environment Variables & Docker Container Deployment

n8n needs to know its externally reachable URL so that it can generate correct webhook and OAuth callback URLs.

### 4.1 Deployment Command

PowerShell:

```powershell
# Stop and remove existing container instance
docker stop n8n
docker rm n8n

# Spin up updated n8n container
# Replace the WEBHOOK_URL value with the current Cloudflare hostname.
docker run -d `
  --name n8n `
  --restart unless-stopped `
  -p 5678:5678 `
  -e WEBHOOK_URL=https://customise-dow-surprising-meet.trycloudflare.com/ `
  -e N8N_RESTRICT_FILE_ACCESS_TO=/files/ `
  -v n8n_data:/home/node/.n8n `
  -v C:\n8n:/files `
  n8nio/n8n
```

### 4.2 Important

A Quick Tunnel hostname changes when a new Quick Tunnel is created. When that happens, update `WEBHOOK_URL` and any external OAuth redirect URI settings to the new hostname.

For a persistent environment, use a **named Cloudflare Tunnel with a custom domain** instead of a Quick Tunnel.

